# Code owners for the enforcement surface.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. A CODEOWNERS entry only requests a
# review from the named owner. It BLOCKS a merge solely when a ruleset turns on
# `require_code_owner_review`, which this repo deliberately does not: the sole
# maintainer cannot approve their own pull request, so requiring it would make
# every self-authored change unmergeable without an admin bypass — which is the
# same bypass the checks ruleset exists to stop relying on. Read these lines as
# routing and as an inventory, never as a gate.
#
# WHY IT IS STILL WORTH HAVING. A contributor's pull request touching any path
# below now automatically requests the maintainer's review instead of depending
# on someone noticing, and the file states — in the repository, where a reader
# looks — which paths are enforcement surface. Enable `require_code_owner_review`
# the day a second maintainer exists; nothing else needs to change.
#
# THE LIST BELOW IS A MIRROR, NOT A SOURCE. The authoritative definition of the
# enforcement-hook surface is `_HOOK_SURFACE_PREFIXES` + `_HOOK_SURFACE_FILES`
# in scripts/hooks/git_push_guard.py, kept exhaustive by
# tests/test_hooks/test_git_push_guard_hook_surface.py, which parses
# .claude/settings.json and fails CI when a wired hook falls outside the fence.
# GitHub cannot read a Python constant, so this file restates it in glob form —
# and a restatement can drift. It is deliberately written as PREFIXES rather
# than as the enumerated filenames: a broad glob keeps matching when a new hook
# lands, where a copied file list silently stops. Over-matching costs a review
# request nobody needed; under-matching costs the notification entirely.

# ON A FORK OR A SEPARATE INSTALL, RE-POINT THE HANDLE BELOW. GitHub requires
# a code owner to have write access, so an upstream handle in someone else's
# repository is INERT — no review is requested and nothing breaks or warns. The
# file cannot be generated out of this problem either: GitHub only reads a
# TRACKED `.github/CODEOWNERS`, so the handle has to be committed by whoever
# owns the repository. Change it in one place, here, and the entries below
# follow. (Reviewed as a deliberate exception to the rule against committing
# install-specific values, 2026-09-09: the value cannot live in generated
# config and still function, and its failure mode elsewhere is silence rather
# than breakage.)

# Everything, as the backstop — a single-maintainer repo has one owner, so the
# specific entries below exist to DOCUMENT the surface rather than to change
# who is asked.
